    <!-- Comprehensive In-Depth Educational SEO Content Section -->
    <article class="wrap game-article-content shaka-article">
      <h2>What Is Shakashaka (Proof of Quilt)?</h2>
      <p>Shakashaka is an elegant, highly rewarding pencil-and-paper logic puzzle invented in 2008 by the renowned Japanese puzzle publisher Nikoli. In Japan and international puzzle circles, the game is affectionately known as <em>Proof of Quilt</em> because the finished board resembles an intricate, patchwork quilt made of interwoven geometric cloth swatches. Unlike pure number-placement games such as Sudoku or path-tracing challenges like Slitherlink, Shakashaka combines numerical deduction with spatial and geometric visualization.</p>
      <p>The puzzle is played on a square or rectangular grid containing white cells and solid black blocks. Certain black blocks are inscribed with an integer from 0 to 4. Your mission as the quilt artisan is to sew right-angled isosceles triangle patches into selected white cells so that every remaining unshaded white area forms a perfect rectangle or square, while strictly respecting all numbered clue constraints.</p>

      <h2>How to Play Shakashaka Online</h2>
      <p>Playing Shakashaka online on PuzzleFeels is designed to be intuitive, responsive, and tactile on any screen:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Select your difficulty:</strong> Choose from Easy (5×5 and 6×6 grids), Medium (7×7 and 8×8 grids), Hard (9×9 and 10×10 grids), or take on the daily UTC puzzle.</li>
        <li><strong>Click or tap white cells:</strong> Tapping any white cell cycles through the available options: Empty → Top-Left (◤) → Top-Right (◥) → Bottom-Right (◢) → Bottom-Left (◣) → Empty.</li>
        <li><strong>Right-click or secondary tap:</strong> Right-clicking a cell reverses the cycle, allowing you to quickly clear or place specific orientations without repeated clicks.</li>
        <li><strong>Satisfy numbered clues:</strong> Ensure that every numbered black square touches the exact number of flat triangle legs indicated by its clue number.</li>
        <li><strong>Form complete white rectangles:</strong> Verify that all remaining white spaces across the entire grid form standalone rectangles or squares without irregular notches, L-shaped bends, or stepped borders.</li>
        <li><strong>Validate your solution:</strong> When all constraints are met, the game automatically celebrates your victory and records your personal best time and move count!</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Understanding the Four Triangle Orientations</h2>
      <p>Every placed triangle is an isosceles right-angled triangle that takes up exactly one half of a grid cell. Its hypotenuse (the diagonal edge) splits the cell from corner to corner at a 45-degree angle. Because a square has four corners, there are exactly four legal orientations:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Top-Left ◤ (Orientation 1):</strong> The right-angled black corner sits at the top-left of the cell. Its flat black legs touch the cell's top edge and left edge. Its diagonal hypotenuse slopes from the top-right corner to the bottom-left corner. The white half of the cell lies at the bottom-right.</li>
        <li><strong>Top-Right ◥ (Orientation 2):</strong> The right-angled black corner sits at the top-right of the cell. Its flat black legs touch the top edge and right edge. Its diagonal hypotenuse slopes from the top-left corner to the bottom-right corner. The white half of the cell lies at the bottom-left.</li>
        <li><strong>Bottom-Right ◢ (Orientation 3):</strong> The right-angled black corner sits at the bottom-right of the cell. Its flat black legs touch the bottom edge and right edge. Its diagonal hypotenuse slopes from the top-right corner to the bottom-left corner. The white half of the cell lies at the top-left.</li>
        <li><strong>Bottom-Left ◣ (Orientation 4):</strong> The right-angled black corner sits at the bottom-left of the cell. Its flat black legs touch the bottom edge and left edge. Its diagonal hypotenuse slopes from the top-left corner to the bottom-right corner. The white half of the cell lies at the top-right.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What Do the Numbers in Shakashaka Mean?</h2>
      <p>Numbered black blocks are the anchor clues that kickstart your deductive reasoning. The number indicates how many of the four orthogonally adjacent cells (top, bottom, left, right) contain a black triangle whose flat, right-angled leg rests directly against that clue block:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Clue 0:</strong> None of the four orthogonally adjacent cells may contain a triangle whose flat leg touches this block. Any adjacent white cells must either be left completely empty or contain a triangle whose hypotenuse or white half faces this clue.</li>
        <li><strong>Clue 1:</strong> Exactly one orthogonally adjacent triangle touches this block with its flat black edge.</li>
        <li><strong>Clue 2:</strong> Exactly two orthogonally adjacent triangles touch this block with their flat black edges.</li>
        <li><strong>Clue 3:</strong> Exactly three orthogonally adjacent triangles touch this block with their flat black edges.</li>
        <li><strong>Clue 4:</strong> All four orthogonally adjacent white cells must contain triangles with their flat black legs touching this block! This immediately determines all four neighbors.</li>
        <li><strong>Unnumbered Black Squares:</strong> Solid black barriers that provide no clue quota. Any number of adjacent triangles (from 0 to 4) may touch an unnumbered block, as dictated by the surrounding white rectangles.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The White Rectangle Rule: Orthogonal vs. 45-Degree Tilted Rectangles</h2>
      <p>The single rule that gives Shakashaka its distinctive charm is the <strong>White Rectangle Rule</strong>. Every continuous region of white space remaining on the board must form a perfect geometric rectangle or square. Crucially, these rectangles can manifest in two distinct forms:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Orthogonally Aligned Rectangles:</strong> These rectangles run parallel to the outer board borders. Their sides are formed by the straight edges of black clue blocks, board perimeters, or the flat non-hypotenuse edges of triangles. For instance, a single 1×1 white cell completely boxed in by black blocks is a valid 1×1 square. Similarly, a 2×3 empty white hallway bounded by black walls forms a valid orthogonal rectangle.</li>
        <li><strong>45-Degree Tilted Rectangles (Diamonds):</strong> When triangles are placed with their diagonal hypotenuses facing inward toward each other, they carve out diamonds and tilted rectangles rotated at exactly 45 degrees. A 45-degree rectangle has four corners, each of which is an exact 90-degree right angle formed by the diagonal slopes. These rotated rectangles must have clean, straight diagonal sides with no missing corners or irregular protrusions.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Any white region that forms an L-shape, a T-shape, a cross, a stepped staircase, or a polygon with more than four vertices is strictly illegal.</p>

      <h2>Step-by-Step Solving Strategies & Deduction Techniques</h2>
      <p>Mastering Shakashaka requires combining local clue deductions with global geometric constraints. Here are several proven techniques used by tournament solvers:</p>
      
      <h3>1. The Clue 4 and Clue 0 Quick Starts</h3>
      <p>Always scan the grid first for extreme clues. A clue <strong>4</strong> requires all four neighbors to place triangles touching the clue. A clue <strong>0</strong> forbids any flat leg contact. If a cell adjacent to a 0 needs to participate in a 45-degree diamond, its orientation must point away from the clue.</p>

      <h3>2. Corner of the Board Traps</h3>
      <p>Inspect the four outer corners of the puzzle grid. If an outer corner cell (such as row 1, column 1) is white, consider what happens if a triangle hypotenuse cuts diagonally through it. If a diagonal slice creates an acute 45-degree corner trapped against the outer wall, that white space can never form a 90-degree rectangle. Therefore, outer grid corners often force the surrounding white rectangle to be upright, or strictly dictate which triangle orientation is permissible.</p>

      <h3>3. Preventing Acute Angle Dead-Ends</h3>
      <p>Every corner of a valid white rectangle must measure exactly 90 degrees. If you place a triangle such that its hypotenuse meets a flat grid wall or another flat triangle leg at a 45-degree angle, you must immediately ask: <em>can this 45-degree corner be expanded into a legal rectangle?</em> If the answer is no, that placement is invalid and must be eliminated.</p>

      <h3>4. Ray Tracing and Hypotenuse Propagation</h3>
      <p>Once a diagonal hypotenuse is placed, the white space along that diagonal must continue straight until it reaches a matching 90-degree turn. You can mentally "trace the ray" along the 45-degree line. The opposite side of the tilted rectangle must run parallel at equal distance. This often forces adjacent cells in the same diagonal corridor to adopt matching orientations.</p>

      <h2>Common Shakashaka Mistakes and How to Avoid Them</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Confusing flat legs with hypotenuses:</strong> Remember that a numbered black square only counts triangles whose <em>flat black leg</em> is flush against the clue. Triangles touching via a corner point or having their hypotenuse facing the clue do not count toward the number.</li>
        <li><strong>Accidental L-shaped white spaces:</strong> Forgetting that two adjacent empty cells will automatically merge into a single region unless separated by a black wall or triangle boundary. If two white rectangles touch along a shared edge, they merge into a polyomino that might not be a rectangle.</li>
        <li><strong>Over-populating the grid with triangles:</strong> Shakashaka is not a puzzle where every cell must be shaded. In many puzzles, more than half of the white cells remain completely empty to form expansive white rooms. Only place a triangle when logically compelled.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Difficulty Levels: Easy, Medium, and Hard</h2>
      <p>PuzzleFeels provides handcrafted Shakashaka puzzles tailored for all skill levels:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Easy (5×5 and 6×6):</strong> Compact grids featuring generous clue densities and straightforward deductions. Perfect for learning the four triangle orientations, practicing clue contact rules, and completing quick 2-to-5 minute daily logic breaks.</li>
        <li><strong>Medium (7×7 and 8×8):</strong> Balanced boards with larger white spaces and intricate 45-degree tilted diamonds. Solvers must chain hypotenuse deductions and evaluate board-edge interactions.</li>
        <li><strong>Hard (9×9 and 10×10):</strong> Expansive master quilts featuring sparse clues, multi-step geometric lookaheads, and interlocking rotated rectangles. A true test of deductive discipline and spatial visualization.</li>
        <li><strong>Daily Challenge:</strong> A fresh challenge board synchronized worldwide at midnight UTC. Compete against yourself, build your daily streak, and track personal best times!</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Cognitive & Logic Benefits of Shakashaka</h2>
      <p>Regularly engaging in spatial logic puzzles like Shakashaka offers remarkable cognitive advantages. Mental rotation exercises stimulate the parietal lobes of the brain, enhancing spatial working memory, pattern recognition, and geometric intuition. Furthermore, working through strict deductive logic without guessing fosters patience, structured problem-solving, and mindfulness, providing a deeply satisfying mental workout.</p>

      <!-- Standard FAQ Section -->
      <section class="shaka-faq-section" aria-label="Frequently Asked Questions">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <FaqList items={faqs} />
      </section>
    </article>

    <!-- Explore More Puzzle Games Section (Spot the Difference Standard) -->
    <ExploreMore currentGameId="shakashaka" />
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  /* Base page layout & typography */
  .shaka-page {
    padding-bottom: 70px;
